WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:06.160
The 70th Annual Primetime Emmy Awards were
held September 17, 2018 in Los Angeles, and

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.340
like all awards ceremonies, they were a wild
mix of good and bad, depending on where you

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.680
were and who you were rooting for.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.360
Relive the night with our picks for the five
best and five worst moments from the show.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.080
Best: Henry Winkler strikes gold

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.300
Henry Winkler, known best as Fonzie from Happy
Days, seems to be a genuinely nice guy who

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.740
deserves all his success.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.060
So it was great to see Winkler's talents shine
again in a big way on the HBO hit Barry, and

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:36.140
it was even better to see him finally take
home an Emmy award for acting after five previous

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:36.640
nominations.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:41.740
According to Winkler, he wrote his acceptance
speech when he was first nominated in 1976,

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:46.020
which became hilariously obvious when he gave
a shout-out to his now-adult children.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.060
“Jed, Zoe, and Max, you can go to bed now
- Daddy won!”

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.900
Worst: Banter isn’t always better

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.210
This year's Emmys opened with a tongue-in-cheek
song about how Hollywood has finally solved

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.360
its diversity problem, complete with the hilarious
talents of a diverse set of stars.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:10.320
“We solved it!”

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.100
The funny, statement-making opening showed
promise for an exciting evening, that is,

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.980
until hosts Colin Jost and Michael Che took
the stage.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.340
The goofy musical act proved to be a hard
act to follow for the SNL Weekend Update hosts,

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.740
who seemed a little too stiff.

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:27.799
Perhaps the hosts felt out of place when plucked
from behind their news desk, or maybe their

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.480
jokes seemed a bit too eager to get a rise
out of the audience.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.680
Whatever the case, this was an opening that
just never quite landed.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.900
That’s incredible.”

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.680
Best: All hail Betty White

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:45.120
One of the most joyous moments of the night
happened when Kate McKinnon and Alec Baldwin

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.020
took to the stage to introduce a woman who
needs no introduction: Betty White.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.670
The audience went wild when White appeared,
giving her a lengthy standing ovation as she

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.009
gave brief remarks, cracked a couple of jokes,
and seemed genuinely in awe of the welcome

00:01:58.009 --> 00:01:58.720
she was met with.

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:03.000
No awards were given, but it was nice to have
a moment of well-deserved recognition for

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.700
everyone’s favorite Golden Girl.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.300
“Thank you so much”

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.800
Worst: The Emmy history bits

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.239
To further emphasize the seven decades of
Emmys history, Che and Jost called up Fred

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.280
Armisen and Maya Rudolph to act as Emmy history
correspondents throughout the show.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.340
This had the potential to be an extremely
funny recurring bit, considering the gag was

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.700
that Armisen and Rudolph apparently didn’t
do any research for the gig.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.660
“So you guys know everything there is to
know about the Emmys.”

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:31.840
“Okay.”

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.860
Unfortunately, the comedic talents of these
two SNL veterans were wasted on seemingly

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.680
pointless bits that were made even more awkward
by Che and Jost’s lack of enthusiasm.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.640
“Once again, out of time.”

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.740
“Oh no!”

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.820
Best: Teddy Perkins’ appearance

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:52.939
Fans of Donald Glover’s Atlanta were delighted
during the Emmys broadcast when Teddy Perkins

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.840
was spotted in the star-studded audience,
even hugging Emmy winner Bill Hader.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.730
Perkins, an eccentric character from the Atlanta
episode aptly titled “Teddy Perkins” quickly

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.360
made a name for himself after appearing in
just one episode.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:09.400
This was due partly to Perkins’ sheer strangeness
and partly to the mystery of who exactly was

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:10.000
playing him.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.640
Seeing Perkins pop up again at the Emmys was
so unexpected that the moment was instantly

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.000
one of the night's big winners.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.540
Worst: The weird presentation format

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.239
If the 2018 Emmys format seemed a little different
than usual, that’s because it was.

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:26.799
Instead of having presenters come out and
banter for a moment before presenting the

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.200
nominees and reading the winner's name, the
2018 nominees’ names came first, followed

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.620
by the introduction of the presenters, who
then bantered and read the winner’s name.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:40.060
It was a nice effort to break up the expected
order of things, but it didn't really work.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:42.560
“...Yeah.”

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.960
Best: The live marriage proposal

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:50.700
Director Glenn Weiss made sure we'd never
forget his 2018 Emmys moment when he accepted

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.640
the Outstanding Directing for a Variety Special
Emmy for his work directing the 2018 Oscars.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.320
Weiss began his remarks by memorializing his
mother, who passed away just two weeks prior,

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:03.730
before shifting his focus to his girlfriend
Jan, who was sitting in the audience.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.320
s “You wonder why I don’t like to call
you my girlfriend?

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.460
Because I want to call you my wife.”

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.200
To add an extra layer of heartwarming joy
to the surprise proposal, Weiss revealed that

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:19.579
he'd gotten permission from his father to
use the same ring his late mother had worn.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:20.900
Talk about stealing the show!

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:23.120
Worst: The Reparation Emmys

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.340
Sometimes good ideas simply don’t live up
to their full potential.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.400
Case in point: Michael Che's "Reparation Emmys"
segment.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.550
In the pre-taped bit, Che attempted to highlight
the lack of diversity in previous Emmy ceremonies

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:36.400
by presenting trophies to various black TV
stars.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.510
Unfortunately, it all seemed undercut by Che's
apparent determination to act like he didn't

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:41.000
need to be there.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.100
Add an ill-timed Bill Cosby reference, and
you have the recipe for a truly lackluster

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.760
segment, despite its potential to be both
statement-making and hilarious.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.520
“Let’s just say I stole it from Bill Cosby.”

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:53.480
“You what?”

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.340
Best: Hannah Gadsby steals the show

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:02.000
Hannah Gadsby's Nanette was 2018's most talked-about
comedy special, so it wasn't exactly surprising

00:05:02.009 --> 00:05:05.720
when she walked out on the Emmys stage to
hand out the award for Outstanding Directing

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:06.860
in a Drama Series.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:11.680
Unsurprisingly, Gadsby sidestepped the usual
teleprompter-driven speech and delivered effortlessly

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.280
funny jokes poking fun at the response to
her special.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.440
“Hashtag ‘Not All Men’ but a lot of
‘em.”

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.960
The whole thing was perfectly capped off when
Stephen Daldry won the award for his work

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:26.139
on The Crown, but wasn't in attendance, leaving
Gadsby to accept on his behalf.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.080
“Uh, I think I just leave now, and that’s…”

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.440
Worst: The Americans' brush with glory

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:37.009
Throughout its six seasons on FX, spy drama
The Americans was a show that both critics

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:40.600
and devoted fans loved, but it seemed like
the Emmys simply never got it.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:45.270
However, its four nominations at the 2018
Emmys suggested a possible final chance at

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.420
glory for one of the best shows of its era.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.360
“We had a job to do.”

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.440
When the show won for both Outstanding Writing
and Outstanding Actor, fans of The Americans

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:58.139
were hopeful that Keri Russell would win an
Emmy for her impressive work on the show.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:01.620
However, Russell lost out to the equally deserving
Claire Foy.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:06.300
The show's Outstanding Drama loss to Game
of Thrones was the final gut punch to fans

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.449
and critics who have rooted for the show for
years, and proved that sometimes popularity

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:11.669
wins over substance.

